## Claim
Text: "The White House blocked a plan to send facemasks to every household in April 2020."
Claim author: Don Moynihan
Claim date: September 17, 2020
Claim origin: https://twitter.com/donmoyn/status/1306614873502224385?s=19

## Initial Q&A
### Was there a specific proposal or plan drafted by any U.S. federal agency or task force in early 2020 to mail or distribute face masks to every household in the United States?
Answer: Yes, there was a specific proposal: the White House scrapped an effort to send hundreds of millions of masks to every U.S. household in April 2020, according to an internal HHS email. A draft press release shows the U.S. Postal Service was poised to distribute masks under a program called "Project America Strong," starting with areas with high case counts.

Source URL: https://www.nbcnews.com/health/health-news/trump-administration-scrapped-plan-send-every-american-mask-april-email-n1240359

### Who (which official, office, or agency) originally conceived or championed the plan to send masks to all U.S. households?
Answer: The plan to send face masks to all U.S. households was originally conceived and championed by the United States Postal Service (USPS). According to the obtained internal documents, the agency drafted a plan to distribute 650 million reusable masks, and then-Postmaster General and CEO Megan J. Brennan stated in a prepared release, "Our organization is uniquely suited to undertake this historic mission of delivering face coverings to every American household in the fight against the COVID-19 virus."

Source URL: https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/postal-services-plan-send-650m-face-masks-americans/story?id=73081928

### What was the intended scale and scope of the mask distribution plan—did it aim to cover all U.S. households, or only those in certain high-risk areas?
Answer: The original mask distribution plan aimed to cover **all U.S. households**, not just those in high-risk areas, though it would have prioritized high-risk areas first. According to the draft press release obtained through a FOIA request, "the Postal Service was poised to distribute the masks to every household," but the masks would "first be distributed to homes in areas with the high numbers of cases at the time, including parishes in Louisiana, as well as 'King County, Washington, Wayne County, Michigan and New York.'" The ultimate goal was to send "hundreds of millions of cloth masks to every U.S. household," with the phased rollout beginning in hotspots before expanding nationwide.

Source URL: https://www.nbcnews.com/health/health-news/trump-administration-scrapped-plan-send-every-american-mask-april-email-n1240359

### In what month and year was the plan to send masks to households most actively considered or prepared for implementation?
Answer: The plan to send masks to households was most actively considered and prepared for implementation in **April 2020**.

Source URL: https://www.businessinsider.com/white-house-blocked-usps-from-sending-face-masks-to-all-households-2020-9

### Did the White House, specifically the Executive Office of the President or the Vice President's office, issue a directive, make a decision, or take an action to stop the implementation of the mask distribution plan?
Answer: Yes, according to the NBC News report, "the White House" made the decision to scrap the plan to send masks directly to every household, as confirmed by an internal HHS email from a senior official. The directive resulted in the masks being distributed to businesses, nonprofits, and state and federal agencies instead.

Source URL: https://www.nbcnews.com/health/health-news/trump-administration-scrapped-plan-send-every-american-mask-april-email-n1240359

### What was the formal reason or justification provided by White House officials for deciding not to proceed with the nationwide mask distribution plan?
Answer: According to the report, a senior administration official told The Washington Post that the White House blocked the plan because officials believed "receiving masks might create concern or panic." Specifically, a Trump administration official stated: "There was concern from some in the White House Domestic Policy Council and the office of the vice president that households receiving masks might create concern or panic." This justification aligns with President Trump's own broader stated approach to the pandemic, as he told journalist Bob Woodward in March 2020, "I wanted to always play it down... I don't want to create a panic."

Source URL: https://www.businessinsider.com/white-house-blocked-usps-from-sending-face-masks-to-all-households-2020-9

### Is there documentation or credible testimony from involved officials that directly links the White House to the decision to shelve the mask mailing plan?
Answer: Yes, there is both documentary evidence and credible testimony directly linking the White House to the decision. The Washington Post obtained thousands of internal Postal Service documents through public-records requests, including a draft press release showing the Postal Service was preparing to send out 650 million masks. Additionally, multiple Trump administration officials confirmed the White House's role, with one senior administration official stating: "There was concern from some in the White House Domestic Policy Council and the office of the vice president that households receiving masks might create concern or panic." These sources provide a direct chain of evidence connecting the White House to the shelving of the mask mailing plan.

Source URL: https://www.businessinsider.com/white-house-blocked-usps-from-sending-face-masks-to-all-households-2020-9

### Did the plan involve the U.S. Postal Service (USPS) as the logistical entity for delivering masks to households?
Answer: Yes, the plan specifically involved the U.S. Postal Service (USPS) as the logistical entity for delivering the masks. The source states, "The White House scuttled a plan to partner with the US Postal Service to send every American a face mask," and that "the Postal Service was preparing to send out 650 million face masks."

Source URL: https://www.businessinsider.com/white-house-blocked-usps-from-sending-face-masks-to-all-households-2020-9

### Were any alternative mask distribution strategies, such as sending masks to specific hotspots or retail stores, proposed or implemented by the White House or other agencies instead of a universal household mailing?
Answer: Yes, instead of mailing masks directly to every household, the White House chose to distribute the masks to nonprofit organizations and state and federal agencies. According to NBC News, "the White House made the decision not to move forward and that, instead, the masks would be manufactured and distributed to businesses, state and federal agencies and nonprofit organizations." An HHS spokesperson reported that 600 million masks were ultimately distributed through these alternative channels under "Project America Strong." Notably, the original plan (before it was scrapped) had actually proposed a hotspot-first rollout strategy: the draft press release indicated that "the masks would first be distributed to homes in areas with the high numbers of cases at the time, including parishes in Louisiana, as well as 'King County, Washington, Wayne County, Michigan and New York.'" Additionally, HHS set up a website where Americans could request a box of 500 masks each, though demand quickly exceeded supply and the program was shut down by July.

