## Final Judgement
**Step 1: Summary of Key Insights**
The fact-check confirms that the United States Postal Service (USPS) developed a plan, codenamed "Project America Strong," to distribute hundreds of millions of reusable face masks to every U.S. household in April 2020. The operation was ready to launch, with a draft press release and a phased rollout starting in high-case areas. However, internal documents and statements from administration officials show the White House made the decision to scrap the direct-to-household mailout, instead choosing to distribute the masks through businesses and agencies. The White House's cited reason was concern that sending masks might cause public panic.

**Step 2: Information Gap**
The evidence is clear and consistent across multiple sources, leaving no significant factual gaps. The core elements of the claim—the existence of the plan, its timeline (April 2020), and the White House's role in blocking it—are well-documented.

**Step 3: Conclusion**
Based on the documented evidence, the claim that "The White House blocked a plan to send facemasks to every household in April 2020" is directly and thoroughly supported by internal government documents and statements from officials. Therefore, the final decision is `supported`.

### Verdict: SUPPORTED

### Justification
Internal documents and statements from Trump administration officials confirm that the U.S. Postal Service had developed a plan, dubbed "[Project America Strong](https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/postal-services-plan-send-650m-face-masks-americans/story?id=73081928)," to deliver 650 million reusable face masks to every U.S. household in April 2020, with a phased rollout beginning in high-case areas. According to [NBC News](https://www.nbcnews.com/health/health-news/trump-administration-scrapped-plan-send-every-american-mask-april-email-n1240359), an internal HHS email and draft press release show the operation was ready to launch when the White House intervened to shelve the direct-to-household effort. A senior administration official told [The Washington Post](https://www.businessinsider.com/white-house-blocked-usps-from-sending-face-masks-to-all-households-2020-9) that "there was concern from some in the White House Domestic Policy Council and the office of the vice president that households receiving masks might create concern or panic." Instead, the masks were distributed through businesses, nonprofits, and state and federal agencies. The documented evidence—spanning FOIA-released Postal Service documents, HHS internal emails, and on-the-record statements from officials—leaves no significant factual gaps, and the claim is fully supported.